## Short answer

Switch it off with the **enable filter**, not the config array. The filter is framework-owned:

```
ntdst_service_admin_ui_enabled
```

## The slug

`{slug}` is not the class name lowercased. Bootstrap strips **every** occurrence of `Service`, then converts camelCase → snake_case, keeping a run of consecutive capitals as one token. `AdminUIService` → `admin_ui`. The docs list this exact case in the slug table.

This matters: the filter is a **deny** filter and it **fails open**. Misspell the slug (`adminui`, `admin_u_i`) and the service boots anyway, silently. If you want to pin the slug for good, declare `metadata()['name']` — it is whitespace-split and lowercased and takes precedence over the derivation.

## What to write in plugin-config.php

Put the `add_filter()` call above the `return [...]`:

```php
<?php
defined('ABSPATH') || exit;

// Staging only — AdminUIService stays off there, on everywhere else.
add_filter('ntdst_service_admin_ui_enabled', function ($enabled) {
    return wp_get_environment_type() === 'staging' ? false : $enabled;
});

return [
    'services' => [
        'admin' => [
            'ntdstheme\\services\\admin\\AdminUIService',
        ],
    ],
];
```

Return the incoming `$enabled` on the other environments. Do not return `true` — that would override a later disable.

## Alternative: no code at all

Precedence runs metadata → filter → database option. Staging has its own database, so you can flip the option there and touch no code:

```
update_option('ntdst_service_admin_ui', '0');
```

Use the filter if you want the decision in version control. Use the option if you want it toggleable from the admin UI.

## One thing worth separating

"Loading on the front end" and "I want it off in staging" are two different problems. The filter fixes the second one only.

If `AdminUIService` registers admin hooks on every request, the framework answer is runtime gating — `if (!is_admin()) { return; }` at the top of `init()`. The docs are explicit that this beats an `admin_only` metadata flag, because the flag gates bootstrap and the class then never exists for AJAX, CLI, or a frontend caller.

Also worth checking: the docs treat admin controllers and admin UI as **sub-components**, not top-level services. If `AdminUIService` is really an admin controller, it belongs inside the owning feature service's `init()`, not in the `services` list.

## Where it goes

Theme, so use the Theme API in `functions.php` (or inside a service's `init()` if the theme has one). Never `add_action('wp_ajax_...')` — the framework routes everything through `ntdst/api_data/{action}`.

## The handler

```php
$theme->apiAction('delete_artwork', function ($data, $params) {
    $id = absint($params['id'] ?? 0);

    if (!$id) {
        return new WP_Error('missing_id', 'Artwork id required', ['status' => 400]);
    }

    // The id is caller-supplied. Confirm it is an artwork before any cap check —
    // otherwise this becomes a generic delete-any-post endpoint.
    if (get_post_type($id) !== 'artwork') {
        return new WP_Error('not_found', 'Artwork not found', ['status' => 404]);
    }

    // Meta capability: false for an Author on someone else's row, true for a
    // user holding delete_others_artworks. This is the real lock.
    if (!current_user_can('delete_post', $id)) {
        return new WP_Error('forbidden', 'Not allowed', ['status' => 403]);
    }

    $result = ntdst_data()->get('artwork')->delete($id);

    if (is_wp_error($result)) {
        return $result;
    }

    return ['deleted' => $id];
}, ['capability' => $cap]);
```

For the coarse `capability` option, resolve it off the post-type object rather than hardcoding a literal — a literal is only correct while `capability_type === 'post'`:

```php
$type = get_post_type_object('artwork');
$cap  = (is_object($type) && is_string($type->cap->delete_others_posts ?? null)
         && $type->cap->delete_others_posts !== '')
    ? $type->cap->delete_others_posts
    : 'delete_others_posts';
```

Run that resolution after the CPT is registered. If the theme wires `apiAction` before `ntdst_data()->register('artwork', ...)`, drop the option and keep only the in-handler `current_user_can('delete_post', $id)` check — that one runs at request time and is the stronger gate anyway.

**Why `delete_post` and not `delete_posts`:** `delete_posts` / `edit_posts` mean "may act on MY OWN posts". Authors hold them. Your requirement is exactly the `_others_` line, and the per-row meta capability enforces it without you re-deriving ownership.

## Do not make it public

Leave `delete_artwork` out of `ntdst/api/public_actions`. A non-public action makes the router's `check_action_permission()` refuse anonymous callers before your handler runs. The framework ships zero public actions; keep it that way for a destructive one.

## Throttle it

Default is 30/60s. Destructive, so tighten it:

```php
add_filter('ntdst/api/rate_limit/delete_artwork', fn() => 5);
add_filter('ntdst/api/rate_window/delete_artwork', fn() => 300);
```

Budget note: a nonce mint consumes the target action's bucket, so a first call costs 2 units of that 5.

## Admin JS

Enqueue the client so `window.ntdstAPI` and the `wp_rest` nonce exist, then call by name:

```javascript
ntdstAPI.call('delete_artwork', { id: artworkId })
    .then(data => console.log('deleted', data.deleted))
    .catch(err => console.error(err.message));
```

Register your admin bundle in `theme-config.php` with `'admin' => true` so it enqueues on `admin_enqueue_scripts` only:

```php
'scripts' => [
    'ntdst-admin' => [
        'src'   => $dist . '/admin.js',
        'admin' => true,
    ],
],
```

The two-step nonce flow (`/wp-json/ntdst/v1/get_nonce` then `/action`) is handled by `ntdstAPI.call()` — you do not write it.

## What this buys you

| Rule | How it is met |
|---|---|
| No raw `wp_ajax_` | `$theme->apiAction()` → `ntdst/api_data/delete_artwork` |
| Nonce + CSRF + auth gate | Router, before the handler runs |
| Authors blocked on others' rows | `current_user_can('delete_post', $id)` meta cap |
| No caller-parameterised post type | Handler pins `'artwork'` |
| Errors as `WP_Error` | Real 4xx status; an error array would ride out as HTTP 200 `success:true` |
| No raw meta / SQL | `ntdst_data()->get('artwork')->delete()` |
| No cache juggling | Core invalidates on delete; the layer keeps no cache |

