## The door

Admin JS, same origin, in-page command → `ntdst_actions()->register()`. Not `ntdst_rest()` (that is for headless or third-party callers), and never a raw `wp_ajax_delete_artwork` hook.

## The floor, and why one gate is not enough

Two things must be true, and they are separate:

1. **Dispatch floor** — `cap_type` on the register options. It bites ahead of the handler and fails closed.
2. **Per-row check inside the handler** — the docs are explicit that the floor sits *alongside* the handler's own check, never replacing it.

Your requirement ("must not delete someone else's") is an *others* capability. `edit_posts` would not do it: it means "may edit my own posts", and Authors and Contributors hold it. Resolve `edit_others_*` off the post-type object, and fail closed if it is empty or not a string.

## Code

**Repository** — all CPT access goes through it. Handlers never touch `ntdst_data()`.

```php
namespace MyProject\Modules\Artwork;

final class ArtworkRepository
{
    private const TYPE = 'artwork';

    private function model(): \NTDST_Data_Model
    {
        if (! ntdst_data()->isRegistered(self::TYPE)) {
            throw new \RuntimeException('artwork type is not registered');
        }
        return ntdst_data()->get(self::TYPE);
    }

    /** Admin edit context: drafts and pending rows must be visible. */
    public function findEditable(int $id): \WP_Post|\WP_Error
    {
        return $this->model()->find($id, 'any');   // publish-only is the default
    }

    public function delete(int $id): true|\WP_Error
    {
        $result = $this->model()->delete($id);
        return is_wp_error($result) ? $result : true;
    }
}
```

`ntdst_data()->get()` returns a clone and registers nothing, so an unregistered name hands you a silently empty model — hence the `isRegistered()` guard.

**Handler** — a plain class. Do not make it a service; DI autowires it.

```php
namespace MyProject\Modules\Artwork;

final class DeleteArtworkHandler
{
    public function __construct(private ArtworkRepository $artworks) {}

    public function __invoke(array $request)
    {
        $id = absint($request['id'] ?? 0);
        if ($id === 0) {
            return ntdst_response()->error('Missing artwork id.');
        }

        $cap = $this->editOthersCap();
        if ($cap === null) {
            return ntdst_response()->error('Not allowed.');   // fail closed
        }

        $post = $this->artworks->findEditable($id);
        if (is_wp_error($post)) {
            return ntdst_response()->error($post->get_error_message());
        }
        if ($post->post_type !== 'artwork') {
            return ntdst_response()->error('Not an artwork.');
        }

        // Per-row check. Someone else's row needs the "others" cap.
        $mine = (int) $post->post_author === get_current_user_id();
        if (! $mine && ! current_user_can($cap)) {
            return ntdst_response()->error('Not allowed.');
        }

        $deleted = $this->artworks->delete($id);
        if (is_wp_error($deleted)) {
            return ntdst_response()->error($deleted->get_error_message());
        }

        return ntdst_response()->success(['id' => $id]);
    }

    private function editOthersCap(): ?string
    {
        $type = get_post_type_object('artwork');
        $cap  = $type->cap->edit_others_posts ?? null;

        return (is_string($cap) && $cap !== '') ? $cap : null;
    }
}
```

**Service** — the thing that hooks into WordPress at boot.

```php
namespace MyProject\Modules\Artwork;

final class ArtworkAdminService implements \NTDST_Service_Meta
{
    public static function metadata(): array
    {
        return ['name' => 'artwork_admin', 'enabled' => true];
    }

    public function __construct(private DeleteArtworkHandler $delete)
    {
        $this->init();      // Bootstrap calls only metadata(); constructor wires hooks
    }

    private function init(): void
    {
        add_action('init', function (): void {
            ntdst_actions()->register('delete_artwork', $this->delete, [
                'cap_type' => 'artwork',   // derived floor, checked at dispatch
            ]);
        });
    }
}
```

No `'public' => true`. Omitting both `public` and a floor would leave it merely login-required, which any Author passes — the `cap_type` floor is what makes it more than that.

List the class in `plugin-config.php` under `services.core`, or put it at the **root** of a `discovery_paths` entry. Auto-discovery does not scan subdirectories.

**Admin asset** — explicit, and through `on()`:

```php
$theme->on('admin_enqueue_scripts', function () use ($theme) {
    $theme->script('artwork-admin', $src, ['jquery'], $ver, true);
});
```

An `'assets'` block in `theme-config.php` enqueues nothing. That key was deleted and fails silently.

## Traps that apply to this exact job

- **Reads are publish-only by default.** Without `find($id, 'any')` a draft artwork returns a `WP_Error` that looks identical to "not found". A denial test must first prove the row is reachable.
- **Never swallow the `WP_Error`.** `return false` loses the reason on both the read and the delete.
- **Do not return the raw `WP_Post`.** `find()` populates `->meta` with every row including `_`-prefixed ones, and `json_encode` serialises `post_password`. The success payload above returns only the id.
- **Gate order is registration → auth → rate limit → nonce.** The CSRF check sits below the limiter deliberately; do not "fix" that.
- **`verifyOrigin()` fails open** with no `Origin`, no `Referer` and no auth cookie. It is CSRF defence for a browser, not an auth gate. The capability checks above are what actually authorise.
- **Never project-prefix** `ntdst_service_artwork_admin_enabled` / `_config`. The enabled filter is a deny filter with a `true` default, so a misspelled slug boots the service you meant to disable.

## One thing to confirm in source

The contract says a `cap_type` floor is "derived from the post type" but does not name *which* capability it derives. If it derives an `edit_posts`-level cap, the floor alone will admit Authors, and the handler's explicit `edit_others_*` check is what enforces your requirement. Read the dispatch code before you trust the floor on its own. If `artwork` still registers with `capability_type => 'post'`, a literal `'capability' => 'edit_others_posts'` is also correct today — but it breaks the moment the type gets its own capability type, which is why the derived form is preferred.

## The one thing to change: which verb you call

Your two-hook shape is right. The verb in the `authenticate` check is the bug.

The limiter has three verbs and they are not interchangeable:

| Call | Does | Belongs on |
|---|---|---|
| `attempt()` | spends **and** decides | a request budget |
| `exceeded()` | asks, spends nothing | a failure counter |
| `reset()` | forgives | a successful login |

A lockout is a failure counter. It gets asked far more often than it gets incremented, so the check must be `exceeded()` and the increment must be separate.

Never check a lockout with `attempt()`. It spends per call, so the check causes the lockout it is checking for.

## Why your double-fire makes this fatal, not just untidy

You noted `authenticate` runs twice per attempt — priority 10 and again after core auth re-asserts. With `attempt()` as the check, one login attempt spends **two** of the three-failure budget before the password is even compared. A user typing the **correct** password on their second try is locked out by the gate, not by any failure.

With `exceeded()` the double-fire costs nothing. Ask it ten times, the count does not move.

## The mapping

```php
// 1. authenticate — ASK ONLY. Runs twice; both runs are free.
add_filter('authenticate', function ($user, $username) {
    if ($username === '' || $this->limiter->exceeded("login:{$username}")) {
        // return the locked-out WP_Error here
    }
    return $user;
}, 30, 3);   // sit above core's own authenticate callbacks

// 2. wp_login_failed — SPEND. This is the only place the count moves.
add_action('wp_login_failed', fn($username) => $this->limiter->attempt("login:{$username}"));

// 3. wp_login — FORGIVE. Without this the window never clears early.
add_action('wp_login', fn($username) => $this->limiter->reset("login:{$username}"));
```

`attempt()` on the failure hook is correct — that hook fires once per real failure, and you discard its return because the decision belongs to `exceeded()` on the next attempt.

The limiter handle itself is not named in the contract doc I have. Resolve it from the source before you write this; the framework names every valid symbol and throws on the rest.

## Two smaller traps in the same code

- **Do not restate the boundary.** The `>= $limit` comparison lives inside `exceeded()`. A call site that also writes `if ($count >= 3)` is a second copy of the rule that will drift.
- **A limit `<= 0` is switched off and can never be exceeded.** Three and fifteen minutes are fine values; a config path that can resolve the limit to `0` silently disables the lockout, and nothing reports it.

## Beyond the limiter

`verifyOrigin()` is not an auth gate here — it returns **true** when there is no `Origin`, no `Referer` and no auth cookie, which is exactly what a scripted login attempt looks like. This handler carries its own authorization; do not lean on origin checking to bound it.

If you wire the lockout config through a service, the override hook is `ntdst_service_{slug}_config` — a project-prefixed name never receives the override from `plugin-config.php`, and the sibling `_enabled` filter **fails open**, so a misspelled slug boots the service you meant to disable.
